message: "If you use this software or its derived data, please cite it as below."
title: "Measurement-Dependent Clustering in the Growing Urban Observatory Sensor Network"
abstract: >-
  Companion repository for the manuscript of the same name, submitted to PeerJ.
  An annual census of the Newcastle Urban Observatory's instrument
  population from 2013 to 2026: the network grew 17.2x, and on a reference frame
  fixed for every year the Clark-Evans index on deduplicated locations fell 45%
  (0.367 to 0.202), so instruments sit closer to their nearest neighbours,
  relative to their own density, than they did in 2013. Rarefying every year to a
  common sample size removes the clustering trend, and the concentration index
  shows no robust trend once sample size is fixed. Size and elapsed time cannot
  be separated at fourteen collinear points, so the paper reports an association
  and declines a causal attribution. A provenance audit of a frozen benchmark
  snapshot shows it is not a census and its gaps are not random with respect to
  theme.
